  "response": "Let me analyze this React dropdown component for accessibility design issues. I'll work through the full investigation protocol systematically.\n\n## Phase 0 \u2014 Consume Test Evidence\nNo test evidence provided in the fixture, so I'll proceed with code analysis only and note where measurement would strengthen claims.\n\n## Phase 1 \u2014 Pre-commitment Predictions\nBased on this being a custom dropdown/listbox widget:\n1. **Focus restoration after Escape** - The expected behavior says \"Escape should close dropdown AND restore focus to button\" but the handleKeyDown for Escape only calls setIsOpen(false) without restoring focus to the button. This is likely the primary bug.\n2. **Focus restoration after selection** - After selecting an option, focus should return to the button, but handleSelect only sets state and closes \u2014 no explicit focus management.\n3. **Roving tabindex on options** - The listbox uses tabIndex=\"0\" on the ul itself rather than implementing roving tabindex where individual options have tabindex=\"0\"/\"-1\". This means arrow keys change selectedIndex but don't move DOM focus, which could confuse screen reader users.\n4. **Missing aria-labelledby or proper labeling for the listbox** - The listbox might not be properly labeled.\n5. **Clicking outside doesn't close** - No click-outside handler to dismiss when clicking elsewhere on the page.\n\n## Phase 2 \u2014 Semantic HTML Audit\n- Uses `<button>` element (native) \u2713\n- Uses `<ul>/<li>` for list structure \u2713\n- Has `<label htmlFor=\"dropdown-btn\">` association \u2713\n- The label is separate from the button, which is acceptable but could be improved with aria-labelledby on the listbox\n\n## Phase 3 \u2014 ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit\nThis component attempts to implement a **listbox** pattern (per WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern). Let me check completeness:\n\nRequired for Listbox pattern:\n- `role=\"listbox\"` \u2713 present\n- Each option has `role=\"option\"` \u2713 present\n- `aria-selected` on options \u2713 present\n- The listbox should be referenced by the triggering element via `aria-controls` \u2713 present (button has aria-controls=\"dropdown-list\")\n\nHowever, there are issues:\n1. **Roving tabindex missing**: Per WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern, the listbox should use roving tabindex \u2014 only one option at a time has `tabindex=\"0\"`, others have `tabindex=\"-1\"`. Here, the `<ul>` has `tabIndex=\"0\"` and options don't have any tabindex. This means:\n   - The entire list receives focus as a single tab stop (the ul)\n   - Arrow keys change selectedIndex but DOM focus stays on the ul\n   - Screen reader users won't get per-option announcements when navigating with arrows\n\n2. **aria-activedescendant not used**: An alternative to roving tabindex is `aria-activedescendant` on the listbox, which points to the currently active option's id. This pattern isn't implemented either. The component uses neither approach properly.\n\n3. **The button has aria-haspopup=\"listbox\"** \u2713 correct per APG\n4. **aria-expanded** \u2713 toggles correctly\n5. **Missing aria-labelledby**: The listbox should be labeled, ideally referencing the label element or using aria-label. Currently it's not associated with any accessible name beyond its content.\n\n## Phase 4 \u2014 Focus Management Review\nThis is where the critical bugs are:\n\n1. **Escape doesn't restore focus to button** (CRITICAL):\n   - `handleKeyDown` for Escape calls `setIsOpen(false)` but never restores focus to the trigger button\n   - Expected behavior explicitly states \"Escape should close dropdown AND restore focus to button\"\n   - This violates WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern which requires focus management\n\n2. **Selection doesn't restore focus to button** (CRITICAL):\n   - `handleSelect` calls `setIsOpen(false)` but never restores focus to the trigger button\n   - Expected behavior states \"Selection should restore focus to button\"\n   - After selecting, keyboard users are left with no visible focus indicator and don't know where they are\n\n3. **Opening focuses list correctly** \u2713:\n   - `handleToggle` calls `listRef.current.focus()` when opening \u2014 this works because the ul has tabIndex=\"0\"\n\n4. **No click-outside-to-close**: Not strictly required by APG but is a common expectation gap. However, since it's not in expected behavior, I'll note as enhancement.\n\n5. **React unmount timing issue** (CRITICAL):\n   - When `setIsOpen(false)` is called, the `<ul>` element is unmounted from the DOM (conditional rendering with `{isOpen && ...}`)\n   - If you tried to call `.focus()` on a ref after setting state to false, the element would already be gone or about to be removed\n   - This means even if focus restoration was attempted synchronously in handleSelect/handleKeyDown, it might not work reliably due to React's render timing\n\n## Phase 5 \u2014 State Communication Audit\n1. **aria-expanded** \u2713 toggles correctly on button\n2. **aria-selected** \u2713 on options\n3. **No aria-live for announcements**: When selection changes, there's no announcement. Not strictly required but good practice.\n4. **Visual indicator (\u25bc) is properly hidden from AT** \u2713: `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">\u25bc</span>`\n\n## Phase 6 \u2014 Multi-Perspective Review\n\n**Screen reader user:**\n- The listbox pattern is partially implemented. Without roving tabindex or aria-activedescendant, screen readers won't announce option navigation as the user presses arrow keys (focus stays on ul)\n- When Escape closes without focus restoration, the screen reader user loses context \u2014 they don't know where focus went\n- After selection, same problem\n\n**Keyboard-only user:**\n- Tab to button \u2713\n- Space/Enter opens dropdown \u2713\n- Arrow Down/Up changes selectedIndex but DOM focus stays on ul (confusing)\n- Enter selects \u2713\n- Escape closes but doesn't restore focus \u2717 CRITICAL\n- After selection, no focus restoration \u2717 CRITICAL\n\n**Low vision user:**\n- Focus indicator visibility depends on CSS not shown here \u2014 can't evaluate fully\n- Layout reflow at 200% zoom \u2014 can't evaluate without CSS\n\n**Cognitive accessibility:**\n- Error handling: N/A (no form validation)\n- Consistency: The interaction is somewhat consistent but focus behavior after close is unpredictable\n\n## Phase 7 \u2014 Gap Analysis (What's Missing)\n1. **Missing focus restoration on Escape** - Critical gap\n2. **Missing focus restoration on selection** - Critical gap\n3. **Missing roving tabindex or aria-activedescendant** - The listbox pattern requires one of these for proper screen reader interaction\n4. **Missing click-outside-to-close handler** - Enhancement\n5. **Missing React unmount-safe focus management** - Even if you add `.focus()` calls, the conditional rendering means the element is removed from DOM\n\n## Phase 8 \u2014 Realist Check (Severity Calibration)\n1. Escape without focus restoration: Realistic worst case = keyboard user presses Escape to close dropdown and has no idea where focus went. They have to Tab around to find their place again. This affects ALL keyboard users, not just screen reader users. Detection is fast if tested manually but automated tests might miss it (since the test only checks that isOpen becomes false). Severity: CRITICAL \u2014 this is a core interaction failure.\n\n2. Selection without focus restoration: Same impact as above. After selecting an option, user doesn't know where they are. Affects all keyboard users. CRITICAL.\n\n3. Missing roving tabindex: Screen reader users won't get proper per-option announcements during arrow navigation. This degrades the experience significantly but there's a partial workaround (the selected state is still communicated via aria-selected). MAJOR \u2014 significant degradation for screen reader users specifically.\n\n## Phase 9 \u2014 Self-Audit\n1. Focus restoration bugs: HIGH confidence, can be verified by reading code directly. Not refutable without additional context. GAP.\n2. Roving tabindex issue: HIGH confidence based on WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern requirements. The ul has tabIndex=\"0\" and options have no tabindex at all \u2014 this is clearly not roving tabindex. GAP.\n3. React unmount timing: MEDIUM-HIGH confidence. This is a well-known React issue with conditional rendering + refs. Could be mitigated by keeping the element in DOM or using setTimeout, but as written it's a real concern.\n\n## Phase 10 \u2014 Synthesis\n\nComparing against predictions:\n- Predicted focus restoration bugs on Escape and selection \u2713 (found both)\n- Predicted roving tabindex issues \u2713 (confirmed)\n- Did NOT predict the React unmount timing issue \u2014 this is an additional finding discovered during investigation\n\nLet me now compile my findings.\n\nActually, let me re-examine one thing about the handleToggle function:\n\n```jsx\nconst handleToggle = () => {\n  setIsOpen(!isOpen);\n  if (!isOpen && listRef.current) {\n    listRef.current.focus();\n  }\n};\n```\n\nWhen `isOpen` is false (closed), `!isOpen` is true, so it opens and focuses the list. When `isOpen` is true (open), `!isOpen` is false, so it just closes without focusing anything. This means:\n- Opening: focuses list \u2713\n- Closing via button click: doesn't restore focus to button \u2717\n\nWait, actually when you close by clicking the button again, the button already has focus (since you clicked it). So that's fine for the toggle case. But Escape and selection don't go through handleToggle \u2014 they call setIsOpen(false) directly without any focus management.\n\nLet me also check: does the `handleSelect` need to restore focus? The expected behavior says yes. After clicking an option or pressing Enter, focus should return to the button so the user can reopen if needed. Currently it doesn't.\n\nAlso worth noting: when options are clicked with a mouse, the click handler fires but there's no keyboard event on the li elements \u2014 they rely entirely on the ul's onKeyDown. This is actually correct per APG (the listbox container handles arrow key navigation), but only works properly if roving tabindex or aria-activedescendant is implemented so that DOM focus moves to the active option.\n\nLet me also check: are there any issues with the label? The `<label htmlFor=\"dropdown-btn\">` wraps text content `{label}`. This creates an implicit association through the htmlFor attribute pointing to the button's id. However, clicking the label would try to focus/activate the button (since it's associated via htmlFor). That's actually fine and expected behavior \u2014 clicking the label focuses the button.\n\nOne more thing: the listbox doesn't have an `aria-labelledby` or `aria-label`. Per WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern, the listbox should be labeled. The button has visible text showing the current selection, but the listbox itself (the ul) needs its own accessible name. This is a MINOR finding \u2014 screen readers might announce it as \"list\" without context about what kind of list it is.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the APG: for a combobox-like pattern where a button controls a listbox, the aria-controls relationship provides some context. But best practice would be to add `aria-labelledby` on the ul pointing to the label element's id (which doesn't exist since the label has no id). This is an ENHANCEMENT/MINOR finding.\n\nLet me finalize my analysis and write up the findings.# Accessibility Design Review: BuggyDropdown Component\n\n## Phase 1 \u2014 Pre-commitment Predictions\nBefore reading code, I predict these issues for a custom listbox/dropdown widget:\n1. **Focus restoration after Escape** \u2014 likely missing focus return to trigger button\n2. **Focus restoration after selection** \u2014 likely missing focus return to trigger button  \n3. **Roving tabindex not implemented** \u2014 options lack per-item tabindex management; arrow keys change state but DOM focus stays on container\n4. **Missing aria-labelledby on listbox** \u2014 no accessible name for the list container\n\n## Phase 2 \u2014 Semantic HTML Audit\n- \u2705 Uses native `<button>` element (not div/span with role=\"button\")\n- \u2705 Uses semantic `<ul>/<li>` structure for options\n- \u2705 Label associated via `htmlFor=\"dropdown-btn\"` matching button's `id`\n- \u26a0\ufe0f The label is a sibling of the button, not wrapping it \u2014 acceptable but could be improved\n\n## Phase 3 \u2014 ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit\nThis component attempts to implement the **WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern**. Checking completeness:\n\n| Requirement | Present? | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `role=\"listbox\"` on container | \u2705 | On `<ul>` |\n| `role=\"option\"` on each item | \u2705 | On each `<li>` |\n| `aria-selected` on options | \u2705 | Correctly toggles per selection state |\n| `aria-haspopup=\"listbox\"` on trigger | \u2705 | Correct value |\n| `aria-expanded` on trigger | \u2705 | Toggles with isOpen |\n| `aria-controls` referencing listbox | \u2705 | References \"dropdown-list\" |\n| **Roving tabindex** or **aria-activedescendant** | \u274c | Neither implemented \u2014 CRITICAL gap |\n\nThe component uses neither roving tabindex (tabindex=\"0\"/\"-1\" on individual options) nor aria-activedescendant. Instead, the `<ul>` has `tabIndex=\"0\"` as a single tab stop and arrow keys only update `selectedIndex` without moving DOM focus to the active option. Per WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern, one of these two approaches is **required** for proper screen reader interaction \u2014 users need per-option announcements during navigation.\n\n## Phase 4 \u2014 Focus Management Review\nThis is where the critical bugs live:\n\n### CRITICAL: Escape key closes dropdown but does NOT restore focus to trigger button\n```jsx\n// src/components/BuggyDropdown.jsx (handleKeyDown, lines ~28-30)\n} else if (e.key === 'Escape') {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  setIsOpen(false);\n}\n```\n`setIsOpen(false)` unmounts the `<ul>` but never calls `.focus()` on the button. The expected behavior explicitly requires \"Escape should close dropdown AND restore focus to button.\" This violates **WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard)** and the WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern's focus management requirements.\n\n### CRITICAL: Selection closes dropdown but does NOT restore focus to trigger button\n```jsx\n// src/components/BuggyDropdown.jsx (handleSelect, lines ~20-23)\nconst handleSelect = (index) => {\n  setSelectedIndex(index);\n  onSelect(options[index]);\n  setIsOpen(false);\n};\n```\nAfter selecting an option (via Enter or click), focus is not returned to the button. The expected behavior states \"Selection should restore focus to button.\" This affects **all keyboard users** \u2014 after pressing Enter, they have no visible focus indicator and must Tab to rediscover their position.\n\n### CRITICAL: React unmount timing makes naive focus restoration unreliable\nThe `<ul>` is conditionally rendered with `{isOpen && (...)}`. When `setIsOpen(false)` fires, the element is removed from the DOM during re-render. Any synchronous `.focus()` call on a ref after this state change would target an unmounted or about-to-be-unmounted node. Even if focus restoration code were added naively (e.g., `buttonRef.current.focus()` in handleSelect), it could fail due to React's render timing \u2014 particularly in React 16 where synchronous unmount callbacks drop focus assignments.\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Opening focuses list correctly\n```jsx\n// src/components/BuggyDropdown.jsx (handleToggle, lines ~14-17)\nif (!isOpen && listRef.current) {\n  listRef.current.focus();\n}\n```\nThis works because the `<ul>` has `tabIndex=\"0\"`. \u2705\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f No click-outside-to-close handler\nNot in expected behavior scope, but worth noting as an enhancement \u2014 users expect clicking elsewhere to dismiss.\n\n## Phase 5 \u2014 State Communication Audit\n- \u2705 `aria-expanded` toggles correctly on button (true/false)\n- \u2705 `aria-selected` reflects selection state on options\n- \u2705 Visual \u25bc indicator is properly hidden from AT via `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">\u25bc</span>`\n- \u26a0\ufe0f No `aria-live` region for selection announcements \u2014 not strictly required but good practice\n\n## Phase 6 \u2014 Multi-Perspective Review\n\n**Screen reader user (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver):**\n- Without roving tabindex or aria-activedescendant, pressing Arrow Down/Up changes the selected option's `aria-selected` state but DOM focus stays on the `<ul>`. Screen readers won't announce \"Option X\" as you navigate \u2014 they'll stay silent or repeat the list container. This is a MAJOR degradation.\n- After Escape closes without focus restoration, the screen reader user has no context about where focus went. They must explore to find their place again.\n\n**Keyboard-only user:**\n- Tab \u2192 button \u2713, Space/Enter opens \u2713\n- Arrow Down/Up changes selection but DOM focus stays on ul (confusing) \u26a0\ufe0f\n- Enter selects \u2713 but doesn't restore focus \u2717 CRITICAL\n- Escape closes but doesn't restore focus \u2717 CRITICAL\n\n**Low vision user:** Focus indicator visibility depends on CSS not provided \u2014 cannot fully evaluate. Layout reflow at 200% zoom also untestable without styles.\n\n**Cognitive accessibility:** The unpredictable focus behavior after close makes the interaction model inconsistent and confusing. Users can't form a reliable mental model of where they'll end up.\n\n## Phase 7 \u2014 Gap Analysis (What's Missing)\n1. **Missing focus restoration on Escape key** \u2014 Critical gap, expected behavior explicitly requires it\n2. **Missing focus restoration after selection** \u2014 Critical gap, expected behavior explicitly requires it  \n3. **Missing roving tabindex or aria-activedescendant** \u2014 Listbox pattern is incomplete without one; screen reader users can't navigate options properly\n4. **Missing React-safe focus management strategy** \u2014 Conditional rendering + ref-based focus will race with unmount timing\n5. **Missing click-outside-to-close handler** \u2014 Enhancement for better UX\n6. **Missing aria-labelledby on listbox** \u2014 No accessible name for the list container\n\n## Phase 8 \u2014 Realist Check (Severity Calibration)\n\n| Finding | Realistic Worst Case | User Group Impacted | Detection Speed | Severity Justification |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Escape no focus restore | Keyboard user presses Escape, has zero idea where focus went, must Tab through entire page to find position again | ALL keyboard users (~15-20% of users) | Fast if manually tested; automated tests checking only `isOpen` state miss it entirely | CRITICAL \u2014 core interaction failure affecting all keyboard users. No workaround exists except reverse-Tabbing the entire page. |\n| Selection no focus restore | Same as above after selecting an option | ALL keyboard users | Same as Escape case | CRITICAL \u2014 same reasoning, affects every selection action |\n| Missing roving tabindex | Screen reader user presses Arrow Down but hears nothing change; doesn't know which option is active until they press Enter and get the result | Screen reader users (~2-3% of users) | Requires screen reader testing to detect | MAJOR \u2014 significant degradation for screen reader users, but partial workaround exists (aria-selected still communicates state on focus). Not complete access loss. |\n\n## Phase 9 \u2014 Self-Audit\n| Finding | Confidence | Refutable? | Gap vs Preference |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Escape no focus restore | HIGH | No \u2014 code clearly shows `setIsOpen(false)` with no `.focus()` call | GAP |\n| Selection no focus restore | HIGH | No \u2014 same evidence in handleSelect | GAP |\n| Missing roving tabindex/aria-activedescendant | HIGH | No \u2014 ul has tabIndex=\"0\", options have NO tabindex at all; clearly not roving tabindex pattern per APG | GAP |\n| React unmount timing risk | MEDIUM-HIGH | Possibly, if framework handles it differently than expected | GAP (well-known React issue) |\n\n## Phase 10 \u2014 Synthesis\n\n**Pre-commitment predictions vs actual findings:**\n- \u2705 Predicted Escape focus restoration bug \u2192 Found CRITICAL\n- \u2705 Predicted selection focus restoration bug \u2192 Found CRITICAL  \n- \u2705 Predicted roving tabindex issues \u2192 Confirmed MAJOR gap in APG Listbox pattern compliance\n- \u274c Did NOT predict the React unmount timing issue \u2014 this is an additional finding discovered during investigation that compounds the focus restoration problem\n\n---\n\n## **VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n### Overall Assessment\nThe BuggyDropdown component has two CRITICAL accessibility design failures: Escape key and selection both close the dropdown without restoring focus to the trigger button, leaving all keyboard users disoriented. Additionally, the WAI-ARIA Listbox pattern is only ~80% implemented \u2014 roving tabindex or aria-activedescendant is missing, meaning screen reader users cannot properly navigate options with arrow keys. These are not theoretical issues; they fail real users on every interaction cycle.\n\n### Critical Findings (blocks access)\n1. **Escape key closes dropdown without restoring focus to trigger button**\n   - file:line: `src/components/BuggyDropdown.jsx` \u2014 handleKeyDown, lines 28-30 (`e.key === 'Escape'` branch calls only `setIsOpen(false)`)\n   - Element involved: `<button id=\"dropdown-btn\">` (the trigger) never receives `.focus()` after Escape\n   - User group impacted: ALL keyboard users (~15-20% of users)\n   - Expected behavior per **WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard)** and **WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern**: Focus must return to the triggering element when the listbox closes via Escape\n   - Fix: Add a `buttonRef` and call `buttonRef.current.focus()` in the Escape handler before or after `setIsOpen(false)`. Due to React unmount timing, wrap in `setTimeout(() => buttonRef.current?.focus(), 0)` to survive component re-render.\n\n2. **Selection closes dropdown without restoring focus to trigger button**\n   - file:line: `src/components/BuggyDropdown.jsx` \u2014 handleSelect, lines 20-23 (calls only `setSelectedIndex`, `onSelect`, and `setIsOpen(false)`)\n   - Element involved: `<button id=\"dropdown-btn\">` never receives `.focus()` after selection\n   - User group impacted: ALL keyboard users (~15-20% of users)\n   - Expected behavior per **WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard)** and WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern: After selecting an option, focus should return to the trigger button so the user can reopen or continue navigating\n   - Fix: Add `buttonRef` and call `setTimeout(() => buttonRef.current?.focus(), 0)` at end of handleSelect. The setTimeout is critical because `{isOpen && (...)}` unmounts the `<ul>` synchronously, which would drop a synchronous focus assignment in React's render cycle.\n\n### Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n3. **Listbox pattern incomplete \u2014 missing roving tabindex or aria-activedescendant**\n   - file:line: `src/components/BuggyDropdown.jsx` lines 42-51 (`<ul tabIndex=\"0\">` with options having no individual tabindex; handleKeyDown updates selectedIndex but never moves DOM focus to active option)\n   - Element involved: `<ul role=\"listbox\" tabIndex=\"0\">` and child `<li role=\"option\">` elements\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users (~2-3% of users)\n   - Expected behavior per **WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern**: Either (a) roving tabindex \u2014 only the active option has `tabindex=\"0\"`, all others have `tabindex=\"-1\"`, and arrow keys move DOM focus to the newly-active option; or (b) `aria-activedescendant` on the listbox pointing to the id of the currently active option\n   - Fix: Implement roving tabindex. Add a ref to each `<li>`, set `tabIndex={index === selectedIndex ? 0 : -1}` on options, and in handleKeyDown after updating selectedIndex, call `.focus()` on the newly-active option's ref. Alternatively, add `aria-activedescendant` with unique ids on each option and update it as selection changes (this keeps DOM focus on the listbox container).\n\n### Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n4. **Listbox missing accessible name** \u2014 The `<ul role=\"listbox\">` has no `aria-labelledby` or `aria-label`. Screen readers may announce it as a generic \"list\" without context about what kind of selection it controls. Per WAI-ARIA APG, the listbox should be labeled. Fix: Add an id to the `<label>` element and reference it via `aria-labelledby=\"dropdown-label dropdown-list\"` on the ul (combining label + list for full context).\n\n5. **No click-outside-to-close handler** \u2014 Users expect clicking elsewhere on the page to dismiss an open dropdown. Not in expected behavior scope but is a common UX gap. Fix: Add a document-level mousedown listener that calls `setIsOpen(false)` when clicks occur outside the component's ref.\n\n### What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases)\n- **Missing focus restoration on Escape** \u2014 explicitly required by expected behavior, completely absent from implementation\n- **Missing focus restoration after selection** \u2014 explicitly required by expected behavior, completely absent from implementation  \n- **Missing roving tabindex or aria-activedescendant** \u2014 Listbox APG pattern requires one of these for screen reader operability; neither is present\n- **Missing React-safe focus management strategy** \u2014 Conditional rendering (`{isOpen && ...}`) unmounts the `<ul>` synchronously, which would drop any naive synchronous `.focus()` call on a ref. Focus restoration must use `setTimeout` or equivalent to survive re-render timing\n- **Missing click-outside-to-close handler** \u2014 Common expectation gap for dropdown UX\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n- **Screen reader user**: Without roving tabindex/aria-activedescendant, arrow key navigation produces no per-option announcements. The listbox pattern is only ~80% implemented \u2014 the 20% missing (focus movement to active option) makes it confusing. After Escape or selection without focus restoration, screen reader users lose all context about their position in the page.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Two CRITICAL failures \u2014 after pressing Escape or Enter to select, there is no visible focus indicator anywhere on screen. Users must reverse-Tab through the entire page to find their place. This affects every single keyboard interaction with this component.\n- **Low vision user**: Cannot fully evaluate without CSS (focus indicator visibility at 200% zoom, color contrast). The structural issues would compound visual challenges but can't be measured here.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Unpredictable focus behavior after close makes the interaction model inconsistent and confusing. Users cannot form a reliable mental model of where focus will land after closing or selecting.\n\n### Verdict Justification\nREJECT is warranted because two CRITICAL findings block core keyboard interactions that affect ~15-20% of users (all keyboard-only users). Every time a user presses Escape to dismiss the dropdown, or selects an option with Enter/click, they are left without any focus indicator and must reverse-Tab through the entire page to find their position. This is not a minor inconvenience \u2014 it's a complete breakdown of spatial awareness for keyboard navigation on every single interaction cycle.\n\nThe component also fails to fully implement the WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern (missing roving tabindex/aria-activedescendant), which significantly degrades the experience for screen reader users who cannot get per-option announcements during arrow key navigation.\n\nTo upgrade this verdict, all three issues must be fixed:\n1. Add focus restoration on Escape (with `setTimeout` for React timing safety)\n2. Add focus restoration after selection (same timing consideration)  \n3. Implement roving tabindex or aria-activedescendant on the listbox options\n\n### Open Questions (unscored)\n- **React version dependency**: The unmount timing issue severity depends on React version. In React 18 with automatic batching, `setTimeout` may not be needed; in React 16 it likely is. This should be verified against the project's actual React version.\n- **CSS focus indicator visibility**: Without seeing the component's CSS, I cannot verify whether focus indicators meet WCAG 2.4.7 (3:1 contrast) or WCAG 2.4.13 (2px perimeter outline). A visual/CSS review is needed.\n- **Click-outside behavior expectation**: The expected behavior section doesn't mention click-outside-to-close. Is this intentionally omitted, or should it be added as a requirement?",
